SHIORI 1

// ---- Interlude: the Keeper's point of view. She is blind, so the screen is black.

# interlude_glass "Glass"
@chapter "Interlude" subtitle="The Keeper"
@pov suzu
@scene black with=fade time=2
@music glass_hill fade=3 volume=0.6
@mode nvl
Night is the loud time.
In the day there's the town: gulls, carts, the bakery bell four hundred and twelve steps down. At night the kiln ticks as it cools, and the Glasshouse starts to sing.
Eleven thousand, four hundred and six beads, and each one hums the note of the thought inside it. Mr. Imai's, from this morning, is a flat E, low and frightened. The schoolteacher's fires are a shrill G-sharp.
Hinata Asahi's beads, four years of Sundays, ring together in a chord. Always the same warm chord, like a kitchen. I've never told her. She wouldn't remember if I did.
I'm writing a song out of them.
It's a silly thing to do with the worst thoughts of four hundred people. But when I tap the right beads in the right order, something comes out that isn't terrible at all. It might be the most beautiful thing in Shiomi. That's the joke of my life: I'm always the only one who hears it.
@clear
Today the glass went past my left wrist, a thumb's width further. I can't feel the bowl when I hold it now. I hear it instead.
Ten summers, the archive says, give or take. I've had nine and the start of a tenth. The glass is at my collarbones. When it reaches the heart I'll be finished: still and clear and pretty, like the girls in the garden. I talk to them sometimes. They never answer. They were very well brought up.
Here is a thought I can't take up the hill, because I am the hill.
{i}Some nights I think about walking down into the town and letting them all break.{/i} Eleven thousand beads going home at once. The kindest town on the coast remembering itself all in one night. I'd stand in the square and listen to their faces.
I'd like that. For a minute I'd like it very much.
I won't. I only think it. There's no Keeper for the Keeper, so I keep my own, and they ring in me like a cracked bell.
@clear
She came today.
She walks the same: left foot first, a little heavy, as if each step has to be talked into it. She still doesn't like cucumber. She still breathes through her mouth when she's thinking.
@if touched
  She took my hand. Rule one, broken before supper on the first day. When she touched me, the bead I keep in my sleeve rang like a struck bell, and I know she heard something. I lay here an hour afterwards, trying to be sorry.
@else
  She offered her sleeve. A model employee. I was proud of her, and I lay here an hour afterwards wishing she hadn't been.
She doesn't know me. That was the bargain, and I'm the one who made it.
In a little pocket sewn into my sleeve is the one bead I never hung. It's always warm. It rings a note I never use in the song, because using it would be cheating.
Goodnight, Nagi.
@clear
@mode adv
-> sunday
